C - STRINGS
============

Store and manipulate text.


STRING BASICS
-------------
char word[10] = "Hello";
char *str = "World";

Strings are arrays of characters.
Ends with \0 (null terminator).


DECLARATION
-----------
char name[20];           Array (fixed size)
char *ptr = "Hello";     Pointer to string


FUNCTIONS
---------
strlen(str)        Length without \0
strcpy(dst, src)   Copy string
strcat(s1, s2)     Combine strings
strcmp(s1, s2)     Compare strings
printf("%s", str)  Print string


EXAMPLE
-------
char greeting[20] = "Hello";
strcat(greeting, " World");
printf("%s\n", greeting);   Prints: Hello World


INPUT
-----
scanf("%s", name);   Read word (careful!)
fgets(name, 20, stdin); Read line (safer)


COMMON ERRORS
-------------
Buffer overflow → Write too much data
Forgot \0 → String not terminated
scanf with %s → Security risk


TIPS
----
Always declare enough size.
Use fgets instead of scanf for input.
Strings are tricky in C.
Be careful with memory.
